Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE, AFTERNOON

Period live-action London: fog presses against the windows. EBENEZER SCROOGE, a thin, rigid old man with a pointed nose, frosted white hair, blue lips, and a black greatcoat, works beneath the sign SCROOGE AND MARLEY.

BOB CRATCHIT, young, mild, and threadbare in a long white comforter, copies letters in a cramped, unheated clerk's cell. One coal glows in his fire.

FRED, Scrooge's ruddy, bright-eyed nephew, bursts in from the cold.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
What's Christmas time to you but a time for paying bills without money; a time for finding yourself a year older, but not an hour richer?

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time; when men and women seem by one consent to open their shut-up hearts freely.

BOB involuntarily applauds. Scrooge glares. Bob pokes his dying fire.

FRED
Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! And a happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves warmly. Two portly charity collectors, GENTLEMAN HARKER and GENTLEMAN VALE, enter with subscription books.

GENTLEMAN HARKER
At this festive season, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

GENTLEMAN VALE
Many can't go to the workhouses; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

GENTLEMAN HARKER
But you might know it.

SCROOGE
It's not my business. Mine occupies me constantly. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Outside, a CHILD CAROLER begins to sing at the keyhole. Scrooge snatches up a ruler; the singing stops.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! Be here all the earlier next morning.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - CHRISTMAS EVE, NIGHT

Scrooge crosses a black yard toward a gloomy old building. His key turns in the lock.

The iron door knocker becomes the livid, spectacled face of JACOB MARLEY.

Scrooge freezes. The face turns back into a knocker.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - CHRISTMAS EVE, NIGHT

A small fire struggles beneath Dutch tiles. Scrooge, in a worn nightcap and dressing-gown, eats gruel beside a disused bell.

The bell begins to swing. First silently, then violently. Every bell in the house answers.

A chain clanks up from the cellar. It comes through the locked door.

MARLEY'S GHOST stands before Scrooge: transparent, in his old pigtail, waistcoat, tights, and boots. A steel chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and purses winds around him.

SCROOGE
What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you!

Marley gives a terrible cry. His bandage falls open; his jaw drops upon his breast. Scrooge falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard.

SCROOGE
You were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were all my business.

Marley lifts his chain. It crashes onto the floor.

MARLEY'S GHOST
You have laboured on your chain since. It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I have none to give. But I am here to warn you. You have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate.

SCROOGE
Thank'ee!

MARLEY'S GHOST
You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Expect the first when the bell tolls One. The second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate.

The window rises. Beyond it, chained phantoms moan over the frozen city.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Remember what has passed between us!

Marley floats backward through the window and vanishes into the night.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS EVE, LATE NIGHT

The bell tolls ONE. White light flashes. Bed curtains pull apart.

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST appears: child-sized and ancient at once, white-haired, in a white tunic with summer flowers and holly. A clear flame shines from its crown.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

SCROOGE
What business brought you here?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your welfare.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

The Spirit touches Scrooge's heart. The bedroom dissolves.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD NEAR SCROOGE'S SCHOOL - WINTER DAY

Snow covers fields. Boys race home for Christmas, calling greetings. Scrooge watches them with tears in his cold eyes.

SCROOGE
I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

INT. SCROOGE'S BOARDING SCHOOL - WINTER DAY

A bare schoolroom. YOUNG EBENEZER, a lonely boy in plain dark clothes, reads beside a feeble fire.

A vivid storybook vision of ALI BABA and an ass passes the window. Young Ebenezer smiles.

SCROOGE
Why, it's Ali Baba! Dear old honest Ali Baba!

His smile collapses into tears.

SCROOGE
Poor boy.

FAN, a delicate little girl with bright eyes and a wool cloak, runs in and flings her arms around Young Ebenezer.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, home, home!

YOUNG EBENEZER
Home, little Fan?

FAN
Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. We're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

The Ghost and Scrooge watch Fan lead her brother away.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She had a large heart.

SCROOGE
So she had. God forbid!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had one child.

SCROOGE
My nephew.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE, YEARS EARLIER

A bustling warehouse transforms into a bright ballroom. FEZZIWIG, a round, laughing old merchant in a Welsh wig and capacious waistcoat, claps his hands.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve! Let's have the shutters up!

YOUNG EBENEZER and DICK WILKINS race to close the shop. A fiddler plays. Workers dance. Fezziwig and MRS. FEZZIWIG whirl through Sir Roger de Coverley.

Scrooge smiles helplessly, moved by the happiness around him.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money.

SCROOGE
It isn't that. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil.

The dancers fade. Scrooge looks down, ashamed.

SCROOGE
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - EVENING, YEARS EARLIER

A younger Scrooge sits opposite BELLE, a gentle young woman in a dark mourning dress. Tears shine in her eyes.

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

YOUNG SCROOGE
There is nothing on which it is so hard as poverty; and there is nothing it professes to condemn with such severity as the pursuit of wealth.

BELLE
You fear the world too much. All your other hopes have merged into the hope of being beyond the chance of its sordid reproach.

YOUNG SCROOGE
I am not changed towards you.

BELLE
You are changed. The master-passion, Gain, engrosses you.

Young Scrooge cannot answer.

BELLE
I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

She leaves him. The present Scrooge turns on the Spirit.

SCROOGE
Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - CHRISTMAS EVE, YEARS LATER

Belle, now a warm, contented matron, sits by a fire surrounded by laughing children. Her HUSBAND enters with parcels.

BELLE'S HUSBAND
I passed his office window. His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear; and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

Scrooge covers his face.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Remove me from this place. I cannot bear it!

He grabs the Spirit's extinguisher-cap and forces it down. Light streams from beneath it.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS NIGHT

Scrooge wakes to a ruddy glow pouring through his open door.

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - CHRISTMAS NIGHT

The sitting room is transformed into a green grove of holly, ivy, mistletoe, fruit, turkeys, puddings, and steaming punch.

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT sits enthroned amid the feast: a huge, genial man with dark brown curls, a green fur-bordered robe, a holly wreath, and a glowing torch.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man!

SCROOGE
I am the Ghost of Christmas Present's pupil. Conduct me where you will. If you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Scrooge holds the green robe. Snow is scraped from pavements; bakers, grocers, and fruit sellers bustle. Families carry dinners through the cold.

The Spirit sprinkles light from his torch onto a poor family's covered dish. Two dinner-carriers bump shoulders, then laugh instead of fighting.

SCROOGE
Would it apply to any kind of dinner on this day?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

SCROOGE
Why to a poor one most?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Because it needs it most.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - CHRISTMAS DAY

A small four-room home, patched but tidy. MRS. CRATCHIT, brave in a twice-turned gown and ribbons, sets the table. MARTHA, BELINDA, PETER, and the YOUNG CRATCHITS bustle around a goose.

The door opens. Bob enters in his white comforter, carrying TINY TIM on his shoulder. Tiny Tim is pale and slight, with a little crutch and iron leg frame.

BOB
And how did little Tim behave?

TINY TIM
As good as gold, and better.

BOB
He told me he hoped the people saw him in church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day, who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

The family gathers around the steaming goose. Then Mrs. Cratchit proudly brings in a blazing plum pudding topped with holly.

BOB
A merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

CRATCHIT FAMILY
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Scrooge stares at Tiny Tim.

SCROOGE
Tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge bows his head beneath his own words.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered what the surplus is, and where it is.

MRS. CRATCHIT reluctantly lifts a small glass.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year!

BOB
My dear. Christmas Day.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - CHRISTMAS NIGHT

Fred and his wife CLARA sit with TOPPER and several friends around a bright fire. They laugh over mulled wine.

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

CLARA
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
His wealth is of no use to him. He don't do any good with it. He don't make himself comfortable with it.

CLARA
I have no patience with him.

FRED
Oh, I have! I am sorry for him. Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always.

The guests begin a yes-and-no game.

TOPPER
Is it a bear?

FRED laughs uncontrollably.

CLARA
I have found it out! It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-o-oge!

The room erupts with laughter. Scrooge nearly laughs with them.

FRED
He has given us plenty of merriment, I am sure, and it would be ungrateful not to drink his health. Uncle Scrooge!

ALL
Uncle Scrooge!

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas and a happy New Year to you!

No one hears him, but his face softens.

EXT. LONDON STREET - MIDNIGHT

The Ghost of Christmas Present has aged; his robe hangs heavily. He opens its folds to reveal two ragged, wolfish children: IGNORANCE and WANT.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The clock strikes TWELVE. The Spirit vanishes.

A tall, hooded figure in a black shroud glides from the mist: the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME. Only one outstretched hand is visible.

SCROOGE
I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But I am prepared to bear you company, and do it with a thankful heart. Lead on!

EXT. ROYAL EXCHANGE - DAY, FUTURE

Businessmen gather in the winter street. Scrooge and the silent Phantom stand unseen nearby.

MR. HAWKINS, a heavy man with a monstrous chin, speaks with MR. POOLE and MR. RIGBY.

MR. HAWKINS
I only know he's dead.

MR. POOLE
When did he die?

MR. HAWKINS
Last night, I believe.

MR. RIGBY
What has he done with his money?

MR. HAWKINS
Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me. That's all I know.

They laugh.

MR. POOLE
It's likely to be a very cheap funeral. I don't know of anybody to go to it.

MR. HAWKINS
I'll offer to go, if anybody else will.

They stroll away, indifferent. Scrooge searches the crowd for himself and does not find him.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG-AND-BONE SHOP - NIGHT, FUTURE

A foul, low shop piled with keys, chains, rags, bones, and refuse iron. OLD JOE, grey-haired and nearly seventy, smokes by a charcoal stove.

MRS. DILBER enters with a bundle. LAUNDRESS BESS follows, then UNDERTAKER'S MAN HOBBS.

MRS. DILBER
If we haven't all three met here without meaning it!

OLD JOE
Come into the parlour.

They throw their bundles down. Joe examines trinkets, sheets, towels, silver spoons, and boots.

LAUNDRESS BESS unrolls dark bed-curtains.

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains!

LAUNDRESS BESS
Yes. Why not?

OLD JOE
You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

LAUNDRESS BESS
Why wasn't he natural in his lifetime? If he had been, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death, instead of lying gasping out his last there, alone by himself.

MRS. DILBER
It's a judgment on him.

LAUNDRESS BESS
He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

Scrooge recoils in horror.

SCROOGE
The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way now.

INT. DEAD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT, FUTURE

A bare, dark bed. Beneath a ragged sheet lies a body, unwept and unwatched. A cat claws at the door; rats gnaw beneath the hearthstone.

The Phantom points to the covered head.

SCROOGE
This is a fearful place. In leaving it, I shall not leave its lesson. Let us go!

The finger does not move.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me!

INT. DEBTOR'S PARLOUR - DAY, FUTURE

CAROLINE waits anxiously with her children. Her husband, THOMAS, enters, careworn but relieved.

CAROLINE
Is it good or bad?

THOMAS
Bad.

CAROLINE
We are quite ruined?

THOMAS
No. There is hope yet, Caroline.

CAROLINE
If he relents, there is!

THOMAS
He is past relenting. He is dead.

Caroline clasps her hands. Relief escapes her before shame overtakes it.

THOMAS
We may sleep to-night with light hearts, Caroline.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY, FUTURE

The same humble room is silent. Tiny Tim's crutch leans ownerless beside the hearth.

Mrs. Cratchit sews, fighting tears. Peter reads from a book. Bob enters slowly in his white comforter.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed.

PETER
And so have I. Often.

BOB
Yes, my dear. I wish you could have gone. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday.

Bob breaks.

BOB
My little, little child! My little child!

He goes upstairs.

INT. TINY TIM'S BEDROOM - DAY, FUTURE

Bob sits beside Tiny Tim's empty little chair. Christmas decorations hang untouched nearby.

BOB kisses the child's still face.

INT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT, FUTURE

An overgrown churchyard, walled in by houses. The hooded Phantom points down at a neglected gravestone.

Scrooge approaches it on trembling knees and reads the inscription: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to Scrooge and back again.

SCROOGE
Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change.

The Spirit remains still.

SCROOGE
I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse.

Scrooge clutches the Spirit's robe.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me!

The grave and Spirit collapse into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Scrooge wakes in his own bed. Sunlight fills the room. Church bells roar outside.

SCROOGE
They are not torn down! They are here! I am here! The shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled!

He laughs and cries, scrambling into his clothes.

SCROOGE
I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy!

He throws open the window. A smartly dressed street boy, TOM, looks up.

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

TOM
To-day! Why, Christmas Day.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one?

TOM
I should hope I did.

SCROOGE
Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey? Not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

TOM
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here. Come back with the man in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

TOM
Walk-ER!

SCROOGE
No, no! I am in earnest!

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS DAY

A massive turkey rides away in a cab toward Camden Town. Scrooge walks among bright-faced Londoners in his best black coat and top hat, beaming at everyone.

GENTLEMAN HARKER approaches. Scrooge steps directly to him.

SCROOGE
My dear sir! I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir!

GENTLEMAN HARKER
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Allow me to ask your pardon.

Scrooge whispers a donation into Harker's ear.

GENTLEMAN HARKER
My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it.

GENTLEMAN HARKER
I don't know what to say to such munificence.

SCROOGE
Don't say anything, please. Come and see me!

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - CHRISTMAS DAY

Fred and Clara inspect a lavish Christmas dinner. The door opens.

SCROOGE
Fred!

CLARA startles. Fred turns, astonished.

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred embraces him with both arms. Clara smiles through tears.

FRED
Let him in? Of course!

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

The morning after Christmas. Scrooge sits at his desk, pretending his old severity. His office fire burns high; a fresh coal-scuttle stands by Bob's cell.

Bob slips in eighteen minutes late, white comforter in hand.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Step this way, sir, if you please.

Bob approaches cautiously.

BOB
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer.

Bob braces himself.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob stares.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas than I have given you for many a year. I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family.

Scrooge claps Bob warmly on the back.

SCROOGE
Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

BOB
God bless you, Mr. Scrooge!

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - CHRISTMAS DAY, LATER

The great turkey fills the Cratchits' little table. Tiny Tim, alive and smiling, sits beside Bob with his crutch.

Scrooge enters carrying parcels. The whole family rises in astonishment.

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Scrooge looks at the child, overcome, then joins the family at the table.

FADE OUT.
